<h2 class="content-box pad-box-mini" style="background-color: #941728; color: #ffffff;">Probability and likelihood answer different questions</h2>
<p>Suppose a small text generator can produce either <strong>yes</strong> or <strong>no</strong>, and &mu;&mdash;the Greek letter <strong>mu</strong>&mdash;is the probability of generating <strong>yes</strong>. If &mu; is fixed at 0.70, a <strong>probability</strong> calculation asks what outputs we might observe. After we actually observe seven <strong>yes</strong> outputs in ten trials, a <strong>likelihood</strong> calculation keeps those ten observations fixed and compares candidate values such as &mu; = 0.50, 0.60, or 0.70. The candidate that makes the observed outputs most plausible has the greatest likelihood.</p>
<p>The probability formula and likelihood formula can contain the same factors. What changes is what we hold fixed. Probability treats the model parameter as known and considers possible data. Likelihood treats the observed data as known and compares possible parameter values.</p>
<p><strong>What you will do:</strong> calculate likelihood and log-likelihood for several possible Bernoulli probabilities, see that both choose the same best value, and connect that value to the observed fraction of 1s.</p>
<p><strong>Video focus (under 10 minutes):</strong> probability versus likelihood, independent-data products, log-likelihood sums, and the Bernoulli MLE.</p>
<h2 class="content-box pad-box-mini" style="background-color: #941728; color: #ffffff;">Independent observations</h2>
<p><strong>Independent observations</strong> means that learning one result does not change the probability assigned to another. Adjacent words in real text are not independent, but repeated runs of this tiny yes/no generator can serve as a useful first model. In Equation (3.1), &theta; stands for any parameter or group of parameters. The uppercase <em>L</em> names the likelihood. The &prod; symbol tells us to multiply the probability of every observed result.</p>
<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; margin: 0.75rem 0;">
<div style="flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; text-align: center; overflow-x: auto;"><math style="font-size: 1.2rem;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block" aria-label="Equation 3.1. The likelihood of theta for observations x one through x n equals the product from i equals one to n of p of x i given theta."><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>&theta;</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>&hellip;</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>&prod;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>|</mo><mi>&theta;</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></div>
<span style="width: 3.5rem; flex: 0 0 3.5rem; text-align: right;" aria-hidden="true">(3.1)</span></div>
<p>Products of many probabilities quickly become extremely small, which is awkward for a computer to represent. A logarithm changes multiplication into addition. Equation (3.2) therefore replaces the product in Equation (3.1) with a sum but chooses the same best value of &theta;. The symbol ℓ is a script lowercase <strong>ell</strong> and names the log-likelihood. The &sum; symbol means to add the terms.</p>
<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; margin: 0.75rem 0;">
<div style="flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; text-align: center; overflow-x: auto;"><math style="font-size: 1.2rem;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block" aria-label="Equation 3.2. The log-likelihood ell of theta equals the logarithm of L of theta, which equals the sum from i equals one to n of the log of p of x i given theta."><mrow><mi>ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>&theta;</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace></mspace><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>&theta;</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>&sum;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mi>log</mi><mspace></mspace><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>|</mo><mi>&theta;</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></div>
<span style="width: 3.5rem; flex: 0 0 3.5rem; text-align: right;" aria-hidden="true">(3.2)</span></div>
<h2 class="content-box pad-box-mini" style="background-color: #941728; color: #ffffff;">Maximum likelihood for a Bernoulli variable</h2>
<p>For Bernoulli data, maximum likelihood chooses the probability of 1 that matches the observed fraction of 1s. Equation (3.3) uses the same notation as Lesson 1: <span aria-label="mu hat sub M L">&mu;̂<sub>ML</sub></span>, read &ldquo;mu hat sub M L,&rdquo; is the maximum-likelihood estimate of &mu;.</p>
<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; margin: 0.75rem 0;">
<div style="flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; text-align: center; overflow-x: auto;"><math style="font-size: 1.2rem;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block" aria-label="Equation 3.3. The maximum-likelihood estimate mu hat sub M L equals one over n times the sum from i equals one to n of x i."><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>&mu;</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>M</mi><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo>&sum;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math></div>
<span style="width: 3.5rem; flex: 0 0 3.5rem; text-align: right;" aria-hidden="true">(3.3)</span></div>
<p>This is the sample mean because Bernoulli observations are coded as zero and one. Adding the observations counts the 1s, and dividing by <em>n</em> converts that count into the observed fraction of 1s.</p>
<p><strong>Brief derivation:</strong> For Bernoulli data,<br>
ℓ(&mu;) = &Sigma;[x<sub>i</sub> log &mu; + (1&minus;x<sub>i</sub>) log(1&minus;&mu;)].<br>
Taking the derivative with respect to &mu; gives<br>
&part;ℓ/&part;&mu; = &Sigma;[x<sub>i</sub>/&mu; &minus; (1&minus;x<sub>i</sub>)/(1&minus;&mu;)].<br>
Setting this to zero and solving yields<br>
&mu;<sub>ML</sub> = (1/n)&Sigma;x<sub>i</sub>.</p>
<h2 class="content-box pad-box-mini" style="background-color: #941728; color: #ffffff;">Negative log-likelihood as loss function</h2>
<p>Machine learning minimizes the <strong>negative log-likelihood (NLL)</strong> rather than maximizing likelihood. Since maximizing ℓ(&theta;) is equivalent to minimizing &minus;ℓ(&theta;), this is the same optimization problem. The demonstration below shows that gradient descent on raw likelihood fails because the gradients vanish due to numerical underflow, while gradient descent on NLL succeeds.</p>
<p>Neural networks call &minus;ℓ(&theta;) the <strong>loss function</strong> and use gradient-based optimizers like Adam or SGD to find parameters that minimize it. For classification tasks, the NLL is called <strong>cross-entropy loss</strong>. For regression with Gaussian noise assumptions, minimizing NLL is equivalent to minimizing <strong>mean squared error</strong>. In both cases, we are performing maximum likelihood estimation via gradient descent on the negative log-likelihood.</p>
<p>This connection is fundamental to modern deep learning: training a neural network is maximum likelihood estimation. The billions of parameters in large language models are optimized by minimizing NLL (cross-entropy) over massive text corpora, which is mathematically equivalent to finding the parameters that maximize the likelihood of the observed training data.</p>
<h2 class="content-box pad-box-mini" style="background-color: #941728; color: #ffffff;">Run the demonstration</h2>
<p>The demonstration shows (1) how likelihood underflows to zero while log-likelihood stays computable, and (2) how gradient descent on negative log-likelihood recovers the maximum likelihood estimate. The demo uses PyTorch to show the connection to modern ML training.</p>
